 true] [17592186065493 1075 17592186553198 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186065493 1078 17592186553198 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186068761 1038 17592186553487 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186068761 1038 17592186553500 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186068761 1038 17592186553501 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186068761 1038 17592186553571 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186068762 1038 17592186553494 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186068762 1038 17592186553495 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186068762 1038 17592186553557 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186068817 1038 17592186553549 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186068916 1038 17592186553505 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186068925 1038 17592186553562 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186069008 1070 17592186045485 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186069008 1071 "What is the quotient of $3x^{3} - 2x^{2} - x - 1$ divided by $x^{2} + x + 1$?" 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186069008 1071 "What is the quotient of $3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-x-1$ divided by ${x}^{2}+x+1?$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186069008 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3x-5 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+x+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-\\phantom{0}x-1\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+3x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-5{x}^{2}-4x-1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,5{x}^{2}-5x-5}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is $3x-5,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $x+4.$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186069009 1070 17592186045487 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186069009 1071 "What is the remainder when $-2x^{4} + x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 1$ is divided by $2x^{2} - x + 5$?" 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186069009 1071 "What is the remainder when $-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+1$ is divided by $2{x}^{2}-x+5?$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186069009 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the $x$ term. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing term with a coefficient of zero, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+1=-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+0x+1\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}+1 \\\\  & 2{x}^{2}-x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+0x+1\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,2{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+0x+1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}-x+5}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is $-{x}^{2}+1,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $x-4.$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186069010 1070 17592186045487 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186069010 1071 "What is the remainder when  $-12{x}^{4}+28{x}^{3}-47{x}^{2}+25x-9$ is divided by $6{x}^{2}-5x+4?$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186069010 1071 "What is the remainder when $-12x^{4} + 28x^{3} - 47x^{2} + 25x - 9$ is divided by $6x^{2} - 5x + 4$?" 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186069010 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2{x}^{2}+3x-4 \\\\  & 6{x}^{2}-5x+4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-12{x}^{4}+28{x}^{3}-47{x}^{2}+25x-\\phantom{0}9\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-12{x}^{4}+10{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,18{x}^{3}-39{x}^{2}+25x-9 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{18{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+12x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-24{x}^{2}+13x-9 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,24{x}^{2}+20x-16}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-7x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is $-2{x}^{2}+3x-4,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $-7x+7.$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186073726 1038 17592186553520 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186078527 1038 17592186553493 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186083193 1038 17592186553564 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186090213 1075 17592186553223 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186090213 1078 17592186553223 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186090218 1075 17592186552887 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186090218 1078 17592186552887 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186092370 1038 17592186553490 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186092370 1038 17592186553535 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186092371 1038 17592186553514 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186092418 1045 "Newton's Method" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186092418 1045 "The Newton-Raphson Method" 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186092418 1050 1500.0 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186092658 1070 17592186045485 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186092658 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x) = x^{3} - x - 1$ in the interval $[1, 2]$. Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_{0} = 1$. Round your answer to three decimal places.$x_{2} \\approx$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186092658 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-x-1$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186092658 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}-x-1)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}-1}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}-1)-({x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}-1)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{0}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)^{3}+1}{3(1)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{2{x}_{1}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{1}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.5)^{3}+1}{3(1.5)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7.75}{5.75} \\\\  & ≈1.348,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186092662 1070 17592186045485 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186092662 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x) = \\sin ((x - 4)^{2}) - (x - 6)$ in the interval $[5, 6]$. Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_{0} = 5.5$. Round your answer to three decimal places.$x_{1} \\approx$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186092662 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡((x-4)^{2})-(x-6)$ in the interval $[5,6].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=5.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186092662 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡((x-4)^{2})-(x-6))^{′}=2(x-4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((x-4)^{2})-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(({x}_{n}-4)^{2})-({x}_{n}-6)}{2({x}_{n}-4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({x}_{n}-4)^{2})-1} \\\\ .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=5.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(({x}_{0}-4)^{2})-({x}_{0}-6)}{2({x}_{0}-4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({x}_{0}-4)^{2})-1} \\\\  & =5.5-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2.25)+0.5}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2.25)-1} \\\\  & ≈5.943,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186093374 1064 "In this lesson, we’ll learn how **Newton’s method** (also known as the **Newton–Raphson method**) constructs such an approximation step by step. Our focus will be on the single-variable case, where the goal is to find a real number $x$ such that $f(x) = 0$.\n\nTo apply Newton’s method, we begin by choosing an initial guess for the root, which we’ll denote by $x_0$. This guess might come from intuition or a rough sketch of the graph.\n\nBelow, we see the graph of $f(x)$ along with the initial guess $x_0$.\n\n![](images/e2/e2d794aa00d7b0d84a70344c89872aa48736182ff6dbf334d1201c0451c215b6.png)\n\nThe idea is to simplify the problem by replacing the curve with a straight line near our guess.\nRecall that the equation of the tangent line $y = F(x)$ to the curve $y = f(x)$ at the point $x = x_0$ is\n\n$$\nF(x) = f(x_0) + f'(x_0)(x - x_0).\n$$\n\nSince $F(x)$ closely approximates $f(x)$ near $x_0$, we solve the simpler linear equation $F(x) = 0$ instead of the original nonlinear equation $f(x) = 0$. This gives us a new estimate for the root that is easier to compute.\n\nTo find where this tangent intersects the $x$-axis, we set $F(x) = 0{:}$\n\n$$\n0 = f(x_0) + f'(x_0)(x - x_0)\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the term $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}({x}_{0})(x-{x}_{0}) & =-f({x}_{0}) \\\\ x-{x}_{0} & =-\\frac{f({x}_{0})}{{f}^{'}({x}_{0})} \\\\ x & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{f({x}_{0})}{{f}^{'}({x}_{0})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis new value becomes our next guess, which we call $x_1$. So, we write\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_0 - \\frac{f(x_0)}{f'(x_0)}.\n$$\n\nIn the figure below, the dashed line is the tangent at $x_0$. We follow it down to the $x$-axis to find our next guess, $x_1$.\n\n![](images/8e/8e19765a4f180f1acc95c6460fb63b95ca9c4af811d13a3035d4e66ba26471a1.png)\n\nTo keep improving our estimate, we now repeat the same process, this time starting from $x_1$ instead of $x_0$. Applying the same idea gives the next guess\n\n$$\nx_2 = x_1 - \\frac{f(x_1)}{f'(x_1)}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e789ff357b039591d7bb00d05e48456f3e11ea172b9f04723a670101c1a1c15a.png)\n\nContinuing this process, we generate a sequence of values \n\n$$\nx_0, \\quad\nx_1, \\quad\nx_2, \\quad\nx_3, \n\\dots\n$$\n\n \nthat, under suitable conditions, converges to a root of $f(x)$.\nAt each step, the next approximation is computed using the update rule\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}.\n$$\n\nIn practice, we stop iterating when the change between consecutive guesses is very small, meaning we’re close enough to the actual root for our purposes." 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186093374 1064 "In this lesson, we’ll learn how **Newton’s method** (also known as the **Newton–Raphson method**) constructs such an approximation step by step. Our focus will be on the single-variable case, where the goal is to find a real number $x$ such that $f(x) = 0$.\n\nTo apply Newton’s method, we begin by choosing an initial guess for the root, which we’ll denote by $x_0$. This guess might come from intuition or a rough sketch of the graph.\n\nBelow, we see the graph of $f(x)$ along with the initial guess $x_0$.\n\n![](images/e2/e2d794aa00d7b0d84a70344c89872aa48736182ff6dbf334d1201c0451c215b6.png)\n\nThe idea is to simplify the problem by replacing the curve with a straight line near our guess.\nRecall that the equation of the tangent line $y = F(x)$ to the curve $y = f(x)$ at the point $x = x_0$ is\n\n$$\nF(x) = f(x_0) + f'(x_0)(x - x_0).\n$$\n\nSince $F(x)$ closely approximates $f(x)$ near $x_0$, we solve the simpler linear equation $F(x) = 0$ instead of the original nonlinear equation $f(x) = 0$. This gives us a new estimate for the root that is easier to compute.\n\nTo find where this tangent intersects the $x$-axis, we set $F(x) = 0{:}$\n\n$$\n0 = f(x_0) + f'(x_0)(x - x_0)\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the term $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}({x}_{0})(x-{x}_{0}) & =-f({x}_{0}) \\\\ x-{x}_{0} & =-\\frac{f({x}_{0})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{0})} \\\\ x & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{f({x}_{0})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{0})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis new value becomes our next guess, which we call $x_1$. So, we write\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_0 - \\frac{f(x_0)}{f'(x_0)}.\n$$\n\nIn the figure below, the dashed line is the tangent at $x_0$. We follow it down to the $x$-axis to find our next guess, $x_1$.\n\n![](images/8e/8e19765a4f180f1acc95c6460fb63b95ca9c4af811d13a3035d4e66ba26471a1.png)\n\nTo keep improving our estimate, we now repeat the same process, this time starting from $x_1$ instead of $x_0$. Applying the same idea gives the next guess\n\n$$\nx_2 = x_1 - \\frac{f(x_1)}{f'(x_1)}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e789ff357b039591d7bb00d05e48456f3e11ea172b9f04723a670101c1a1c15a.png)\n\nContinuing this process, we generate a sequence of values \n\n$$\nx_0, \\quad\nx_1, \\quad\nx_2, \\quad\nx_3, \n\\dots\n$$\n\n \nthat, under suitable conditions, converges to a root of $f(x)$.\nAt each step, the next approximation is computed using the update rule\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}.\n$$\n\nIn practice, we stop iterating when the change between consecutive guesses is very small, meaning we’re close enough to the actual root for our purposes." 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186094285 1075 17592186094286 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186094285 1075 17592186094287 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186094285 1075 17592186094288 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186094285 1075 17592186094289 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186094285 1075 17592186094290 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186094285 1075 17592186552962 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186094285 1075 17592186553079 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186094285 1075 17592186553250 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186094285 1075 17592186553329 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186094285 1075 17592186553335 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186094285 1078 17592186552962 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186094441 1075 17592186094442 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186094441 1075 17592186094443 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186094441 1075 17592186094444 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186094441 1075 17592186094445 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186094441 1075 17592186094446 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186094441 1075 17592186552871 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186094441 1075 17592186552923 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186094441 1075 17592186553111 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186094441 1075 17592186553133 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186094441 1075 17592186553353 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186094441 1078 17592186553133 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186095261 1075 17592186095262 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186095261 1075 17592186095263 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186095261 1075 17592186095264 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186095261 1075 17592186095265 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186095261 1075 17592186095266 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186095261 1075 17592186552911 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186095261 1075 17592186552951 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186095261 1075 17592186553201 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186095261 1075 17592186553274 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186095261 1075 17592186553340 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186095261 1078 17592186553340 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186096558 1071 "Calculate the radius $r$ of the cylinder below given that its volume is $200\\pi \\,\\textrm{mm}^3$ and its height is $2\\,\\textrm{mm}$.\n\n![](images/d0/d0450b9c0f3ab8b6f5f0cf09c4ca21dac75d0077fe8b44bf740eb4dc198decfe.png)" 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186096558 1071 "Calculate the radius $r$ of the cylinder below given that its volume is $200\\pi \\,\\textrm{mm}^3$ and its height is $2\\,\\textrm{mm}.$\n\n![](images/d0/d0450b9c0f3ab8b6f5f0cf09c4ca21dac75d0077fe8b44bf740eb4dc198decfe.png)" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186096558 1074 "The volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2h\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nV = 200\\pi \\,\\textrm{mm}^3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nh = 2\\,\\textrm{mm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula and solving for $r$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= πr^{2}h \\\\\n200π &= πr^{2}(2) \\\\\n2πr^{2} &= 200π \\\\\nr^{2} &= 100 \\\\\nr &= 10mm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186096558 1074 "The volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2h.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $V = 200\\pi \\,\\textrm{mm}^3$ and $h = 2\\,\\textrm{mm}$ into the formula and solving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\ 200\\pi  & =\\pi {r}^{2}(2) \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{2} & =200\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =100 \\\\ r & =10\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186096559 1071 "Calculate the volume of a cylinder given that the circumference of the base is $10\\pi \\,\\textrm{m}$ and the ratio of its height to its radius is $\\dfrac{4}{5}$." 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186096559 1071 "Calculate the volume of a cylinder given that the circumference of the base is $10\\pi \\,\\textrm{m}$ and the ratio of its height to its radius is $\\dfrac{4}{5}.$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186096559 1074 "First, we find the radius $r$ using the formula $C=2\\pi r$, where $C$ is the circumference, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= 2πr \\\\\n10π &= 2πr \\\\\nr &= 5m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the height $h$, we use the fact that the ratio of the height to the radius is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{h}{r} &= \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n\\frac{h}{5} &= \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\nh &= 4m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2h\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nr = 5\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nh = 4\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we get\n\n$$\nV = \\pi(5)^2(4) = 100\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^3\n$$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186096559 1074 "First, we find the radius $r$ using the formula $C=2\\pi r,$ where $C$ is the circumference, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi r \\\\ 10\\pi  & =2\\pi r \\\\ r & =5\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the height $h,$ we use the fact that the ratio of the height to the radius is $\\dfrac{4}{5} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{h}{r} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{h}{5} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ h & =4\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2h .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $r = 5\\,\\textrm{m}$ and $h = 4\\,\\textrm{m}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\nV = \\pi(5)^2(4) =  100\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^3 .\n$$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186096560 1071 "Find an expression for the height $h$ of a cylinder in terms of its volume $V$ given that the ratio of the radius of the base to the height of the cylinder is $4$." 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186096560 1071 "Find an expression for the height $h$ of a cylinder in terms of its volume $V$ given that the ratio of the radius of the base to the height of the cylinder is $4.$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186096560 1074 "Using the fact that the ratio of the radius $r$ of the base to the height $h$ is $4$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{r}{h} &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to express $h$ in terms of $V$. Therefore, we need to isolate $r$ in the above equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{r}{h} &= 4 \\\\\nr &= 4h\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting $r = 4h$ into the formula and solving for $h$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= πr^{2}h \\\\\nV &= π(4h)^{2}h \\\\\nV &= 16πh^{3} \\\\\nh^{3} &= \\frac{V}{16π} \\\\\nh &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{V}{16π}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186096560 1074 "Using the fact that the ratio of the radius $r$ of the base to the height $h$ is $4,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to express $h$ in terms of $V.$ Therefore, we need to isolate $r$ in the above equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =4 \\\\ r & =4h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h.\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting $r = 4h$ into the formula and solving for $h,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\ V & =\\pi {(4h)}^{2}h \\\\ V & =16\\pi {h}^{3} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =\\frac{V}{16\\pi } \\\\ h & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{V}{16\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186096594 1071 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $4$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nConsider the data sets above. You are given that $\\overline{x}=4$ and $\\overline{y}=3.$ Find the equation of the corresponding linear regression line. Round the slope and the $y$-intercept to $1$ decimal place if necessary." 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186096594 1071 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $4$ | 