ession line's coefficients to three decimal places and your final answer to the nearest tenth of a centimeter." 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186097078 1071 "| **Age ($x$)** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Height ($y$)** | $95$ | $106$ | $110$ | $125$ |\n\nThe table above gives the ages, in years, and the corresponding heights, in centimeters, of Milly and Rasheed's four children. You are given that $x^(―) = 6$ and $y^(―) = 109$.\n\nFind the equation of the corresponding linear regression line and use this to predict the children's height when they are $6$ years old. Round the regression line's coefficients to three decimal places and your final answer to the nearest tenth of a centimeter.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186097078 1074 "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(3-6)(95-109)+(5-6)(106-109) \\\\  & +(7-6)(110-109)+(9-6)(125-109) \\\\  & =(-3)(-14)+(-1)(-3)+(1)(1)+(3)(16) \\\\  & =42+3+1+48 \\\\  & =94\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(3-6)^{2}+(5-6)^{2}+(7-6)^{2}+(9-6)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+1+9 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=\\frac{94}{20}=\\frac{47}{10}=4.7.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =109-4.7\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =109-28.2 \\\\  & =80.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=4.7x+80.8.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can predict the height of a six-year-old child in this family by substituting $x=6$ into our regression line:\n\n$$\ny=4.7(6)+80.8=109\n$$\n\nTherefore, the model predicts that a six-year-old child will be $109.0\\,\\text{cm}$ tall." 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186097222 1070 17592186045487 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186097222 1071 "Calculate the circumference of the base of a cylinder given that its volume is $1536πcm^{3}$ and the ratio of its radius to its height is $\\frac{1}{3}$." 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186097222 1071 "Calculate the circumference of the base of a cylinder given that its volume is $1\\,536\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ and the ratio of its radius to its height is $\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186097222 1074 "Using the fact that the ratio of the radius $r$ of the base to the height $h$ is $\\frac{1}{3},$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ h & =3r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $V=1\\,536\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ and $h=3r$ into the formula, we can find the value of $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\ 1\\,536\\pi  & =\\pi {r}^{2}(3r) \\\\ 1\\,536 & =3{r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =512 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{512} \\\\ r & =8\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the formula for the circumference of the base is $C=2\\pi r.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nC=16\\pi \\,\\text{cm}.\n$$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186097875 1064 "The volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2h\n$$\n\nLet's use this formula to calculate the volume of the cylinder below:\n\n![](images/be/bef69292f30200f32b78a41f9c0b7585fe35da964ce7f35a594f6d127812e790.png)\n\nFor this cylinder, the radius\n\n$$\nr = 3\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nand the height\n\n$$\nh = 5\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for $V$, we get\n\n$$\nV = \\pi (3)^2 (5) = 45\\pi\\,\\textrm{cm}^3\n$$\n\nWe often express the volume of a cylinder as a multiple of $\\pi$. However, we can also use a calculator to get a numerical approximation for the volume. This gives\n\n$$\nV\\approx 141.37\\,\\textrm{cm}^3\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186097875 1064 "The volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2h.\n$$\n\nLet's use this formula to calculate the volume of the cylinder below:\n\n![](images/be/bef69292f30200f32b78a41f9c0b7585fe35da964ce7f35a594f6d127812e790.png)\n\nFor this cylinder, the radius $r = 3\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and the height $h = 5\\,\\textrm{cm}.$ Substituting these values into the formula for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\nV = \\pi (3)^2 (5) = 45\\pi\\,\\textrm{cm}^3.\n$$\n\nWe often express the volume of a cylinder as a multiple of $\\pi.$ However, we can also use a calculator to get a numerical approximation for the volume. This gives\n\n$$\nV\\approx 141.37\\,\\textrm{cm}^3,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186097888 1064 "Recall that the volume $V$ of a cylinder of radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nThis formula is very intuitive, which makes it easier to remember.\n\nNotice that the base of the cylinder is a circle of radius $r$. Therefore, the area of a circular base is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nLet's label this area on a diagram.\n\n![](images/76/76c1c3179f73ee8464c187d539cebb2692b02763167a21a83857126620338154.png)\n\nTherefore, to compute the volume of the cylinder, we simply multiply the area of the base by the height:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= A \\cdot h \\\\\n&= πr^{2} \\cdot h \\\\\n&= πr^{2}h\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186097888 1064 "Recall that the volume $V$ of a cylinder of radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h.\n$$\n\nThis formula is very intuitive, which makes it easier to remember. \n\nNotice that the base of the cylinder is a circle of radius $r.$ Therefore, the area of a circular base is given by \n\n$$\nA = \\pi r^2.\n$$\n\nLet's label this area on a diagram.\n\n![](images/76/76c1c3179f73ee8464c187d539cebb2692b02763167a21a83857126620338154.png)\n\nTherefore, to compute the volume of the cylinder, we simply multiply the area of the base by the height:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =A\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\pi {r}^{2}h\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186097892 1064 "For a set of $n$ paired observations $x_i$ and $y_i$, the trend line is the straight line that best fits the data.\n\n![](images/a2/a23a1411b40f723b19446d0838346dd74dd7bf75da918859cc688171e808b44a.png)\n\nFinding the equation of the trend line is called **linear regression**. The trend line is commonly called the **regression line** or **line of best fit.**\n\nWe can show that the equation of the trend line is given by\n\n$$\ny = mx+b\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- The slope $m$ is given by\n$m = \\frac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}$.\n- The $y$-intercept $b$ is given by\n$b = \\overline{y} - m \\overline{x}$.\n- $\\overline{x}$ and $\\overline{y}$ are the means of the data sets:\n$\\overline{x} = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i, \\qquad \\overline{y} = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n y_i$\n\n- The quantities $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are given by\n$S_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y})$.\n\nThere's some nice intuition behind the formula for $b$. The trend line *always* passes through the point $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y})$. Therefore, this point must satisfy the trend line equation\n\n$$\ny = mx+b\n$$\n\nWe can then solve this equation for $b$.\n\nAlso, notice that $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are the summation terms that feature in the formulas for the variance and covariance, respectively." 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186097892 1064 "For a set of $n$ paired observations $x_i$ and $y_i,$ the trend line is the straight line that best fits the data.\n\n![](images/a2/a23a1411b40f723b19446d0838346dd74dd7bf75da918859cc688171e808b44a.png)\n\nFinding the equation of the trend line is called **linear regression**. The trend line is commonly called the **regression line** or **line of best fit.**\n\nWe can show that the equation of the trend line is given by\n\n$$\ny = mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- The slope $m$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $y$-intercept $b$ is given by\n\n$$\nb = \\overline{y} - m \\overline{x}.\n$$\n\n- $\\overline{x}$ and $\\overline{y}$ are the means of the data sets:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} =  \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i, \\qquad \\overline{y} =  \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n y_i\n$$\n\n- The quantities $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are given by\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \n\\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y}).\n$$\n\nThere's some nice intuition behind the formula for $b.$ The trend line *always* passes through the point $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y}).$ Therefore, this point must satisfy the trend line equation $y = mx+b.$ We can then solve this equation for $b.$\n\nAlso, notice that $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are the summation terms that feature in the formulas for the variance and covariance, respectively." 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186098331 1078 17592186098334 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186100881 1038 17592186553519 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186100883 1038 17592186553560 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186100928 1038 17592186553524 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186100937 1038 17592186553488 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186101166 1070 17592186045485 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186101166 1071 "Consider the following sequence:\n\n$2, 0,-2,-4,-6, …$\nThe recursive formula for this sequence is given by\n$a_{n + 1} = a_{n} + A, a_{1} = A$.\nFrom left to right, what are the missing values?" 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186101166 1071 "Consider the following sequence: \n\n$$\n2,\\,0,\\,-2,\\,-4,\\,-6,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe recursive formula for this sequence is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,,\\,{a}_{1}=\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing values?" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186101166 1074 "Notice that the first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=2.$\n\nInspecting the terms of the sequence, we see that we always get the next term by subtracting $2.$ This is the same as adding $-2.$\n\nTherefore, the recursive rule must be the following:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+(-2)\\,,\\,{a}_{1}=2\n$$\n\nSo, the missing values are $-2$ and $2.$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186105806 1038 17592186553559 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186105850 1038 17592186553531 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186107607 1075 17592186107608 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186107607 1075 17592186107609 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186107607 1075 17592186107610 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186107607 1075 17592186107611 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186107607 1075 17592186107612 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186107607 1075 17592186552982 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186107607 1075 17592186553184 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186107607 1075 17592186553249 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186107607 1075 17592186553287 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186107607 1075 17592186553303 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186107607 1078 17592186553249 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186110659 1038 17592186553590 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [175