} |  |  |" 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186221995 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=A\\,{e}^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & (2t+1){e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186222007 1070 17592186045485 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186222007 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+3y(t)-{y}^{2}(t)-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186222007 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = x^{2}(t) + 2x(t) + y(t) - 1; y^{′}(t) =-2x(t) + 3y(t) - y^{2}(t) - 2$\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0, 1]^{T}$. Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186222007 1074 "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t),\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+(v(t)+1)-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-2x(t)+3y(t)-{y}^{2}(t)-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =-2u(t)+3(v(t)+1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =-2u(t)+3v(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)-2 \\\\  & =-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186222500 1064 "Suppose we have a system of ODEs such as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = \\mathbf{f}(x(t),y(t))\n$$\n\nwith a nonzero equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ satisfying\n$\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast) = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nInstead of analyzing the system near a *nonzero* equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ we can introduce *shifted coordinates* so that the equilibrium becomes the origin.\nWe define the **deviation vector**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nso that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{0} \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.\n$$\n\nThis transformed system has several advantages: shifting the equilibrium to the origin makes the system easier to analyze without altering its behavior.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following system of ODEs:\n\n![](images/47/4788b3521295a9a2ccdd0840940ff1c27263bb580ce72d787a06e1e1af50b9af.svg)\n\nThe equilibrium point of the system is $[1, \\, -1]^T.$ What is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](images/04/04406c96333cbaabb72b49ad48aac2b144e2f21c01be688c1ad5f0cb6eda5391.svg)\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n![](images/a7/a7d302836d0991995492abd0ccb35bb2701836fe4a4f50decfdd0866e7fecfae.svg)\n\nNotice that for linear systems, shifting the equilibrium to the origin just removes the constant term." 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186222500 1064 "Suppose we have a system of ODEs such as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = \\mathbf{f}(x(t),y(t))\n$$\n\nwith a nonzero equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ satisfying\n$\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast) = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nInstead of analyzing the system near a *nonzero* equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ we can introduce *shifted coordinates* so that the equilibrium becomes the origin.\nWe define the **deviation vector**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nso that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{0} \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.\n$$\n\nThis transformed system has several advantages: shifting the equilibrium to the origin makes the system easier to analyze without altering its behavior.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following system of ODEs:\n\n![](images/47/4788b3521295a9a2ccdd0840940ff1c27263bb580ce72d787a06e1e1af50b9af.svg)\n\nThe equilibrium point of the system is $[1, \\, -1]^T.$ What is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](images/04/04406c96333cbaabb72b49ad48aac2b144e2f21c01be688c1ad5f0cb6eda5391.svg)\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n![](images/a7/a7d302836d0991995492abd0ccb35bb2701836fe4a4f50decfdd0866e7fecfae.svg)\n\nNotice that for linear systems, shifting the equilibrium to the origin just removes the constant term." 13194139535987 17592186045417 false] [17592186228946 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}-3y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-3L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $\\mathcal L\\left\\{y' \\right\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y' \\right\\}  = sY(s) - y(0)   = sY(s) + 2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a