\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)(-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t-3}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}) \\\\  & =u(t-3)(\\frac{3t-10}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}.\n$$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186317324 1074 "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+3)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s+3)+B(s+3)+C{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(3)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(9)\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\